<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lidzbark Warmiński</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Heilsberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lidzbark Warmiński (German Heilsberg) is a town in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Its brick Gothic castle, built in the 14th century, was the residence of the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Warmia for more than four centuries, until the region passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1772. Among those who lived there were the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a>, as secretary to his uncle Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucas-watzenrode">Lucas Watzenrode</a>, and the poet-bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ignacy-krasicki">Ignacy Krasicki</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lidzbark-warminski.jpg" alt="Lidzbark Warmiński" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: image/photo was taken by Wikipedia and Wikimedia Commons user Ludwig Schneider. … · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lidzbark_Warmi%C5%84ski_Zamek_Biskup%C3%B3w_Warmi%C5%84skich_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lidzbark Warmiński, in German Heilsberg, is a historical town in the Warmian-Masurian Voivodeship of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the capital of Lidzbark County. The Elma, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyna">Łyna</a>, flows through it. It was once the capital of Warmia and formerly its largest town, a religious and cultural centre known as the Pearl of Warmia. For a long period it was under the control of the Warmian bishops, and it was also a major economic centre, giving up its importance only to the nearby city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braniewo">Braniewo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town began as a settlement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Old Prussians</a> known as Lecbarg, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> conquered it in 1240 and named it Heilsberg. In 1306 it became the seat of the Bishopric of Warmia, and it received town privileges in 1309. The castle of the Warmian bishops was built in the 1350s and expanded in the following centuries. In 1440 the town joined the anti-Teutonic Prussian Confederation, at whose request King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a> incorporated the region into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> in 1454. That led to the Thirteen Years' War, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn">Second Peace of Thorn</a> (1466) the Teutonic Order gave up its claim.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a> first visited the town at the turn of 1495 and 1496 and lived at the castle from 1503; he is believed to have written part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-revolutionibus-orbium-coelestium">De revolutionibus orbium coelestium</a> there. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII of Sweden</a> resided in the town in the winter of 1703–04 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>. In the mid-18th century Prince-Bishop Adam Stanisław Grabowski found a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the Gesta principum Polonorum, the oldest medieval Polish chronicle, in the castle and had it printed for the first time.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed the region in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772, ending the Prince-Bishopric of Warmia, though the last Prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ignacy-krasicki">Ignacy Krasicki</a>, kept his seat in the town until 1795. Afterwards the town lost the cultural significance it has not regained. It was heavily damaged when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> took it in 1945, and on 19 May 1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> was transferred to Polish authorities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The main landmark is the Gothic Castle of the Warmian Bishops, with its fortifications, towers and the Baroque Grabowski Palace. It houses a museum and is a Historic Monument of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Other sights include the Gothic collegiate church of Saints Peter and Paul, the medieval town walls and High Gate, and the Krasicki Orangery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lidzbark Warmiński” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The province took in Pomerelia with the mouth of the Vistula and the city of Danzig, the Chełmno and Michałów Lands with Thorn, the Prince-Bishopric of Warmia with Frauenburg, <b>Heilsberg</b> and Allenstein, and the Marienburger Land with Marienburg and the city of Elbing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The bishops made <b>Lidzbark</b> their residence and the cathedral chapter settled at Frombork.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-warmia">Prince-Bishopric of Warmia</a></li></ul></section>
